@node Overview
@chapter Overview

Vacuum scans disk usage in parallel, groups the reclaimable space into clear
categories, and lets you delete what you choose. It is a command-line tool with
an interactive terminal user interface (TUI), built to be equally comfortable
for a human at a prompt and for an AI agent driving it over a pipe.

Run with no arguments in a terminal to launch the TUI; otherwise use the
@command{scan}, @command{list}, and @command{clean} subcommands, plus
@command{empty-trash} to reclaim space from items already sent to the trash. Two
further subcommands, @command{schema} and @command{describe}, let a program
discover the command surface without parsing help text.

Vacuum is conservative by default: it never removes anything without an explicit
@option{--apply}, it routes deletions to the trash unless you ask to purge, and
it refuses to touch protected system paths. @xref{Safety Model}.

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

@quotation Warning
Do not run @samp{cargo install vacuum}. An unrelated crate on @uref{https://crates.io,
crates.io} holds that name (a spaceflight command-line tool). Install from the
Vacuum repository, as described below.
@end quotation

@section Nix and NixOS

The flake supplies its own Rust toolchain, so nothing is needed on the host
beyond Nix itself with flakes enabled.

@example
nix run github:Spacecraft-Software/Vacuum
nix profile install github:Spacecraft-Software/Vacuum
nix build github:Spacecraft-Software/Vacuum
@end example

For a system-wide install, add the flake as an input and enable the NixOS
module it exports:

@example
@{
  inputs.vacuum.url = "github:Spacecraft-Software/Vacuum";

  # in your NixOS configuration:
  # imports = [ inputs.vacuum.nixosModules.default ];
  # programs.vacuum.enable = true;
@}
@end example

Equivalently, place the package in @code{environment.systemPackages}
directly:

@example
environment.systemPackages =
  [ inputs.vacuum.packages.$@{pkgs.system@}.default ];
@end example

To work on Vacuum itself, @command{nix develop} provides rustc, cargo, clippy,
rustfmt, rust-analyzer, mold, @command{reuse}, and Texinfo.
@command{nix flake check} builds the package and runs the test suite.

@section GNU Guix System

The repository ships @file{manifest.scm}, which provides the toolchain without
installing anything on the host.

@example
git clone https://github.com/Spacecraft-Software/Vacuum
cd Vacuum
guix shell -m manifest.scm -- cargo install --path crates/vacuum-cli
@end example

This installs the @command{vacuum} binary into @file{~/.cargo/bin}. To obtain
only the development environment:

@example
guix shell -m manifest.scm
guix shell --pure -m manifest.scm
@end example

A Guix package definition exists at @file{packaging/guix.scm}, but it is not
yet buildable. Guix's @code{cargo-build-system} resolves dependencies through
@code{#:cargo-inputs} rather than from @file{Cargo.lock}, and this workspace
locks 191 crates; that list is generated with @samp{guix import crate -r
vacuum} at release time. Until then, use the @command{guix shell} route above.

@section Arch Linux

A @file{PKGBUILD} is provided:

@example
cd packaging && makepkg -si
@end example

@section From source

Vacuum requires Rust edition 2024 with a minimum supported Rust version of
1.85.

@example
cargo install --path crates/vacuum-cli --force
@end example

The @option{--force} flag replaces any previously installed @command{vacuum}
binary.

@node Invocation
@chapter Invocation

@menu
* Subcommands::          scan, list, clean, empty-trash, schema, describe, tui.
* Global Options::       Flags accepted by every subcommand.
* Configuration::        Persistent default scan roots.
@end menu

@node Subcommands
@section Subcommands

@table @code
@item vacuum
Launch the interactive TUI (when standard output is a terminal and no AI agent
is detected). Equivalent to @command{vacuum tui}.

@item vacuum scan [@var{paths}@dots{}] [--root @var{path}@dots{}] [--limit @var{n}]
Report the largest consumers under each path (default: your home directory),
largest first. Equivalent in spirit to @samp{du --max-depth=1 | sort -rh}.
Directories may be given positionally or with @option{--root} (the two are
merged), for parity with @command{list} and @command{clean}.

@item vacuum list [--category @var{cat}@dots{}] [--root @var{path}@dots{}]
List reclaimable candidates by category. Read-only.

@item vacuum clean [--category @var{cat}@dots{}] [--root @var{path}@dots{}] [--apply] [--purge] [--yes]
Remove reclaimable space. Without @option{--apply} this is a dry run.
Deletions go to the trash unless @option{--purge} is given. Use @option{--yes}
(alias @option{--force}) to skip the confirmation prompt; this is required for
non-interactive use. @xref{Safety Model}.

@item vacuum empty-trash [--apply] [--yes]
Permanently empty the operating-system trash, reclaiming the space held by
items previously sent there by @command{vacuum clean} (or anything else).
Without @option{--apply} this is a dry run that only lists what would be
removed; @option{--yes} (alias @option{--force}) skips the confirmation prompt
and is required for non-interactive use. This operation is irreversible and is
available on Linux/Freedesktop and Windows only. @xref{Safety Model}.

@item vacuum schema
Emit a JSON Schema (Draft 2020-12) of the whole command surface on standard
output. The per-command @code{parameters} objects drop directly into the
function-calling APIs of the major LLM providers. @xref{Agent Integration}.

@item vacuum describe
Emit a compact capability manifest: the command list, category slugs, and the
safety defaults. Honors @option{--json}.

@item vacuum tui [@var{paths}@dots{}] [--root @var{path}@dots{}]
Launch the interactive TUI explicitly. Roots may be given positionally or with
@option{--root} (merged), exactly as for @command{scan}; with none, the
configured or default roots are used (@pxref{Configuration}). Inside the TUI,
press @key{r} to add or remove scan roots without relaunching.
@xref{Interactive TUI}.
@end table

@node Global Options
@section Global Options

@command{list} and @command{clean} additionally accept the selection flags
below.

@table @option
@item --category @var{slug}
Restrict to a category; repeatable. Defaults to all. @xref{Cleaner Categories}.

@item --root @var{path}
Operate within @var{path}; repeatable. Defaults to the configured roots.

@item --cleaner @var{id}
Restrict to a single cleaner by identifier; repeatable. Defaults to all.

Finer-grained than @option{--category}, because several cleaners may contribute
to one category and their candidates can deliberately overlap. For a Cargo
project, @samp{--cleaner cargo-prune} takes only the per-unit prune and leaves
the whole-directory candidate alone. @command{vacuum describe} lists the
identifiers.

@item --stale-days @var{n}
Days of disuse after which a build artifact counts as stale; 30 by default.
Overrides the @code{stale_days} configuration key. @xref{Configuration}.
@end table

The remaining flags are accepted by every subcommand.

@table @option
@item --json
Emit the machine-readable JSON envelope. Shorthand for @option{--format json}.

@item --format @var{fmt}
Select the output format: @code{human}, @code{json}, @code{jsonl}, or
@code{explore} (the interactive TUI). @xref{Output Modes}.

@item --fields @var{f1,f2,@dots{}}
Restrict JSON records to the named fields, reducing payload size for
token-billed agents. @xref{Output Modes}.

@item --color @var{when}
@itemx --no-color
Control ANSI color: @code{auto}, @code{always}, or @code{never}. @option{--no-color}
is equivalent to @option{--color never}.

@item --verbose
@itemx -v
Emit diagnostic detail to standard error.

@item --quiet
@itemx -q
Suppress non-error standard-error output, such as per-item skip warnings.

@item --config @var{path}
Read default scan roots from this TOML config file (environment variable
@env{VACUUM_CONFIG}). @xref{Configuration}.

@item --accessible
@itemx --no-accessible
Turn accessible mode on or off for the interactive frontend, overriding
@env{SPACECRAFT_A11Y}, the configuration file and the automatic hints.
@xref{Accessible mode}.

@item --term-protocols @var{list}
Force terminal protocols on or off for the interactive frontend, for example
@samp{--term-protocols=-all} or @samp{--term-protocols=-keyboard,+progress}.
@xref{Terminal protocols}.
@end table

Timestamps in JSON output are always ISO@tie{}8601 UTC with a mandatory @code{Z}
suffix (Standard @t{§14}); local-time rendering never appears in machine output.

@node Configuration
@section Configuration

Vacuum needs no configuration: with no paths given it scans your home directory.
To avoid retyping the same roots, record them once in a TOML config file. The
@code{roots} key is a list of directories; a leading @code{~} expands to your
home directory.

@example
# ~/.config/vacuum/config.toml
roots = ["~/projects", "~/Downloads", "/data/scratch"]
stale_days = 30
@end example

An @code{[accessibility]} table with @code{enabled = true} turns accessible
mode on; @code{enabled = false} keeps it off even when a hint would turn it on
(@pxref{Accessible mode}).

The @code{stale_days} key sets how long a build artifact must go untouched
before it counts as stale, and defaults to 30. It is overridden by
@option{--stale-days} on the command line. Unrecognised keys are ignored rather
than rejected, so a file written for a newer Vacuum still loads on an older
binary.

The default scan roots are resolved with the following precedence, highest
first:

@enumerate
@item
Paths or @option{--root} given on the command line.
@item
The @env{VACUUM_ROOTS} environment variable (an OS-path-separated list, e.g.
@samp{~/a:~/b} on Unix).
@item
The @code{roots} key of the config file.
@item
Your home directory (@env{HOME}).
@end enumerate

The config file is read from @option{--config}, else @env{VACUUM_CONFIG}, else
@file{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/vacuum/config.toml}, else
@file{~/.config/vacuum/config.toml}. A missing file at the default location is
not an error; a path named explicitly with @option{--config} must exist. These
defaults apply only to the scanning commands (@command{scan}, @command{list},
@command{clean}, and @command{tui}); @command{empty-trash} operates on the OS
trash and ignores them.

@node Interactive TUI
@chapter Interactive TUI

Run @command{vacuum} with no arguments in a terminal, or @command{vacuum tui}
explicitly, to launch the interactive frontend. It scans the configured or
default roots (@pxref{Configuration}); pass roots positionally or with
@option{--root} to scan elsewhere, for example
@samp{vacuum tui /spacecraft-software}.

The frontend lists reclaimable candidates grouped by category, with a running
total of the current selection. Deletions are bounded by the same roots and the
same safety guards as the command-line @command{clean} (@pxref{Safety Model}):
choosing a root in the picker can never widen that boundary past what the CLI
itself would allow.

@section Keys

@table @kbd
@item @key{UP}/@key{DOWN}, k/j
Move the cursor.
@item @key{SPC}
Toggle the highlighted candidate.
@item a
Toggle all candidates.
@item y
Copy the highlighted candidate's path (the root of a batch, or the label of a
command) to the clipboard. In the directory picker, copy the highlighted
directory. The copied text is always shown in the status line as well, so you
can copy it by hand in a terminal without OSC@tie{}52.
@item r
Open the directory picker to choose scan roots (see below).
@item p
Toggle purge mode (permanent deletion) versus trash mode (recoverable).
@item @key{RET}
Apply: delete the selected candidates after a confirmation prompt.
@item q, @key{ESC}
Quit.
@end table

@section Directory picker

Press @key{r} to browse the filesystem and change the scanned roots without
relaunching. The picker opens in the current working directory.

@table @kbd
@item @key{UP}/@key{DOWN}, k/j
Move the cursor.
@item @key{RET}, l
Descend into the highlighted directory (the first row, @file{..}, ascends).
@item h, @key{BACKSPACE}
Go to the parent directory.
@item @key{SPC}
Add the highlighted directory as a scan root, or remove it if already chosen; a
@samp{✓} marks the active roots.
@item d
Remove the highlighted directory from the root set.
@item @key{ESC}, q
Close the picker and re-scan with the updated roots.
@end table

Symbolic links are not followed, and unreadable directories are skipped.

@section Progress

Scanning and deleting run on a background thread, so the interface stays
responsive and shows a live progress overlay instead of freezing. Scanning
displays an animated spinner naming the category in progress; deleting adds a
determinate bar with the item count and bytes reclaimed of the selected total.
While a job runs, @key{ESC} (or @kbd{q}) cancels a deletion after the current
item completes, or quits during a scan (scanning is read-only, so its worker is
safely abandoned). A finished deletion re-scans automatically.

@node Terminal protocols
@section Terminal protocols

The frontend uses three modern terminal protocols where the terminal supports
them, and sends nothing where it does not:

@table @asis
@item kitty keyboard protocol
Unambiguous key reporting, so @key{ESC} is recognized at once rather than
after a timeout. Vacuum asks the terminal at startup and uses it only when the
terminal answers. In WezTerm it is off by default; set
@code{config.enable_kitty_keyboard = true} in @file{wezterm.lua}.

@item OSC@tie{}52 clipboard
How @kbd{y} copies. It works over SSH and through tmux (with
@samp{set -g set-clipboard on}). No terminal says whether it accepted a copy,
so the copied text is always shown too.

@item OSC@tie{}9;4 progress
Scan and delete progress in the terminal's tab, title bar or taskbar. Some
terminals show @samp{OSC 9} as a desktop notification instead, so Vacuum sends
it only to terminals known to show progress: Windows Terminal, ConEmu,
Ghostty, VS Code, Rio, contour, foot, WezTerm builds from 2025-02-10 on,
iTerm2@tie{}3.6 or later and kitty@tie{}0.47 or later. Inside tmux, zellij or
GNU screen it is skipped.
@end table

Vacuum shows no images and has no mouse regions, so it does not use the kitty
graphics protocol, animation, rich clipboard or pointer shapes.

Detection runs once at startup: Vacuum writes its queries followed by a DA1
query, which every terminal answers, and gives up after 200@tie{}ms. Nothing is
sent and nothing is probed when standard output is not a terminal, under an AI
agent or CI (@pxref{Agent Integration}), or when @env{TERM} is @code{dumb} or
unset. On exit, on @code{SIGTERM}, @code{SIGHUP} or @code{SIGINT}, and on a
crash, Vacuum restores the keyboard mode and clears the progress indicator
before leaving the alternate screen.

To override detection, pass @option{--term-protocols} or set
@env{SPACECRAFT_TERM_PROTOCOLS} to a comma-separated list: @samp{+name} forces a
protocol on, @samp{-name} forces it off, and @code{all} names every protocol.
The names are @code{keyboard}, @code{graphics}, @code{animation},
@code{clipboard}, @code{clipboard-rich}, @code{progress} and @code{pointer}.
Items apply left to right, and the flag beats the variable name by name:

@example
vacuum tui --term-protocols=-all                # no protocol at all
SPACECRAFT_TERM_PROTOCOLS=+progress vacuum tui  # trust this terminal
@end example

With @option{--verbose}, Vacuum prints the state of every protocol, and why,
after the frontend exits.

@node Accessible mode
@section Accessible mode

Accessible mode (Standard @t{§18}) replaces the animated spinner with a static
@samp{Working@dots{} 40%} line, since screen readers pronounce spinner glyphs.
It is off unless you turn it on. In order of precedence:

@enumerate
@item
@option{--accessible} or @option{--no-accessible};
@item
@env{SPACECRAFT_A11Y} set to @code{1} or @code{0};
@item
@code{enabled} in the @code{[accessibility]} table of the configuration file;
@item
the hints @env{NO_COLOR} (set and non-empty) and @samp{TERM=dumb}, which turn
it on when nothing above has spoken.
@end enumerate

@option{--verbose} reports the resolved state and what decided it.

The frontend still redraws the screen. For a fully linear, screen-reader
friendly path, use the non-interactive commands, which cover every operation
the frontend offers: @command{vacuum list} to review and @command{vacuum clean}
to act (@pxref{Subcommands}).

@node Output Modes
@chapter Output Modes

Vacuum renders the same data two ways. It chooses automatically, but you can
force the choice with @option{--format} or @option{--json}.

@table @asis
@item Detection cascade
An explicit @option{--format}/@option{--json} flag always wins. Otherwise, if an
AI-agent or CI environment variable is set (@pxref{Agent Integration}), or
standard output is not a terminal, Vacuum emits JSON; an interactive terminal
gets human-readable text.

@item The JSON envelope
Every @option{--json} response is a single document of the form:

@verbatim
{
  "metadata": {
    "tool": "vacuum",
    "version": "0.1.0",
    "command": "vacuum list --json",
    "timestamp": "2026-06-21T14:30:00Z",
    "maintainer": "Mohamed Hammad <Mohamed.Hammad@SpacecraftSoftware.org>",
    "website": "https://Vacuum.SpacecraftSoftware.org/"
  },
  "data": { "categories": [ ... ], "total_bytes": 0 }
}
@end verbatim

Property names are @code{snake_case}; numbers are JSON numbers; absent optional
fields are omitted rather than emitted as @code{null}. Output is pretty-printed
only when standard output is a terminal; piped consumers receive compact JSON.

@item JSONL streaming
@option{--format jsonl} emits one JSON record per line with no enclosing
envelope, so a consumer can process records as they arrive. For @command{list}
the rows are candidates; for @command{scan}, usage entries; for @command{clean},
outcomes.

@item Field selection
@option{--fields label,bytes} projects each JSON record down to the named keys.
This is the primary lever for trimming token cost when an agent needs only part
of each record.
@end table

@node Exit Codes
@chapter Exit Codes

Vacuum follows the Spacecraft Software canonical exit-code map, so a calling
program can branch on the status without parsing output.

@table @code
@item 0
Success.
@item 1
General failure (for example, a deletion or external command failed).
@item 2
Usage error: bad arguments. Fix the invocation; do not retry verbatim.
@item 3
Resource not found.
@item 4
Permission denied, or an action refused by a safety guard (outside the allowed
roots, a protected system path, or a symlink).
@item 5
Conflict (already exists).
@item 127
A required external tool is not installed.
@end table

In any machine-readable mode, a non-zero exit is always accompanied by a
structured error object on standard error. @xref{Errors and Hints}.

@node Errors and Hints
@chapter Errors and Hints

In JSON mode, failures are emitted to standard error as a single object so an
agent never has to parse a prose sentence:

@verbatim
{
  "error": {
    "code": "UNKNOWN_CATEGORY",
    "exit_code": 2,
    "message": "unknown category: not-a-category",
    "hint": "vacuum schema --json | jaq '.commands[] | select(.name==\"list\") | .categories'",
    "timestamp": "2026-06-21T14:30:00Z",
    "command": "vacuum list --category not-a-category",
    "docs_url": "https://Vacuum.SpacecraftSoftware.org/"
  }
}
@end verbatim

The @code{hint} field is a @emph{runnable command}, not advice in prose: an agent
can execute it directly to recover. In human mode the same failure renders as a
short @samp{vacuum: @dots{}} line followed by a @samp{hint: @dots{}} line.
Standard output is never written on the error path.

@node Cleaner Categories
@chapter Cleaner Categories

@table @asis
@item Dev build artifacts (@code{build-artifacts})
Regenerable build output: Rust @file{target/} (with a sibling
@file{Cargo.toml}), @file{node_modules}, @file{dist}, @file{build},
@file{__pycache__}, and similar.

This category additionally offers a @emph{prune} of dead build units from
@emph{inside} a Cargo @file{target/} directory you wish to keep, so that a
project still in use retains its warm incremental cache instead of paying for a
full rebuild. The prune is performed natively: Vacuum never executes
@command{cargo}, @command{rustup}, or @command{rustc}, which matters on a system
whose toolchain is provided by Nix or Guix rather than rustup, where there may be
no runnable compiler on @env{PATH} at all.

Three kinds of candidate are produced per target directory:

@table @asis
@item Units from a superseded toolchain
Every build unit records the compiler that produced it, as a hash written by
Cargo into @file{.fingerprint/@var{unit}/*.json}. Units are grouped by that
value; the group with the most recent activity is the toolchain in use, and the
remaining groups are left over from a compiler no longer building this project.
Where the version can be identified it is named (@samp{2 units from rustc
1.95.0}); otherwise the group is described generically, and is pruned just the
same.

@item Cold units
Units built by the toolchain still in use, but not rebuilt within
@option{--stale-days} days (30 by default). Freshness is taken from the
modification time of the @file{invoked.timestamp} file Cargo writes for exactly
this purpose. Access time is deliberately never consulted: on a @code{relatime}
mount --- the near-universal default --- it is not updated when Cargo reuses an
artifact, so an access-time filter deletes caches that are still live.

@item The incremental compilation cache
Pure rebuild-time state, untracked by fingerprints, and frequently the largest
single item in a target directory. Removing it costs one non-incremental
rebuild.
@end table

Several guards apply. A profile whose @file{.cargo-lock} is held by a running
build is skipped in its entirety. The unhashed final artifacts at a profile root
are never removed. A unit whose fingerprint cannot be read is kept rather than
pruned, so unrecognised data is never destroyed. Sizes count a hardlinked inode
once, so the figure reported is the space actually recovered.

These candidates cover the same tree as the whole-directory candidate for that
target, so take one or the other rather than both. @xref{Global Options}, for
@option{--cleaner}, which selects a single cleaner within a category.

@item Package-manager garbage (@code{package-gc})
Garbage collection run through each tool: @command{nix-collect-garbage -d} for
the user profile and, as a @command{sudo} line, for every profile system-wide;
@command{flatpak uninstall --unused}; @command{journalctl --vacuum-time=7d} and
@command{journalctl --vacuum-size=200M}; @command{systemd-tmpfiles --clean};
and container pruning. Offered only when the tool is installed.

@item App / user caches (@code{caches})
@file{~/.cache}, per-application Flatpak caches, and Electron/Chromium caches.

@item Stale temp files (@code{temp-files})
Entries directly inside @file{/tmp}, @file{/var/tmp}, and @env{TMPDIR}. This is
the safe counterpart to @samp{rm -r /tmp/*}, which would destroy other users'
files and the working state of running processes. An entry is offered only when
every one of the following holds: it is not a symbolic link; it is owned by the
invoking user; it has not been modified for seven days; and its name is not
session state such as @file{.X11-unix}, @file{.ICE-unix},
@file{systemd-private-*}, @file{snap-private-*}, or @file{.Trash-*}.

Root-owned leftovers are deliberately excluded, and are reclaimed instead
through @command{sudo systemd-tmpfiles --clean}, offered under
@code{package-gc}.

Selecting this category widens the delete allowlist to cover the temporary
directories, which lie outside the default home-directory root. Every other
guard, including the protected-prefix list and the symlink refusal, continues to
apply. @xref{Safety Model}.

@item Large files (@code{large-files})
The biggest individual files, surfaced for manual review.
@end table

Several cleaners may contribute to one category; output is always grouped by
category, so each category appears exactly once regardless of how many cleaners
fed it.

@node Safety Model
@chapter Safety Model

Vacuum is conservative by design:

@itemize @bullet
@item Dry run by default; nothing is removed without @option{--apply}.
@item Path removals go to the XDG trash unless @option{--purge} is given. The
one exception is a candidate whose trash directory would sit on the same
filesystem as the candidate itself, which is the case for everything under
@file{/tmp}: the freedesktop specification places it in
@file{/tmp/.Trash-$uid}, so trashing would reclaim no space at all. Such
candidates are purged instead, and the reason is stated in the output of every
mode rather than applied silently. @option{--apply} is still required.
@item A real @option{--apply} run refuses to act non-interactively unless
@option{--yes} (alias @option{--force}) is supplied; interactively it prompts.
@item Operation is bounded to your home directory plus any paths you name;
protected system locations are refused. A cleaner may declare additional
locations it needs (the temp-file cleaner declares the temporary directories),
and those are added to the allowlist only when that cleaner is actually
selected.
@item Symlinks are never followed when deleting, and source files are never
removed.
@item Commands that require root are never escalated; the exact @command{sudo}
command is reported for you to run.
@end itemize

@node Agent Integration
@chapter Agent Integration

Vacuum is designed to be driven by an AI agent or an automation pipeline as
readily as by a person.

@table @asis
@item Environment detection
When any of @env{AI_AGENT}, @env{AGENT}, or @env{CI} is set to a non-empty
value, Vacuum suppresses the TUI, defaults to JSON, and disables color. The
informational variables @env{CLAUDECODE}, @env{CURSOR_AGENT}, and
@env{GEMINI_CLI} are recognized but do not by themselves change the output
format.

@item Function-calling schema
@command{vacuum schema} emits a JSON Schema whose per-command @code{parameters}
objects are valid Anthropic @code{input_schema} / MCP @code{inputSchema}
payloads. An agent can fetch the schema once and call the tool without guessing
flag names.

@item Token economy
Use @option{--fields} to trim each record to the keys you need, and
@option{--format jsonl} to stream large result sets. Optional fields are omitted
when absent, and piped output is compact.
@end table

